  "title": "The Great Stink: How a Bad Smell Saved London",
  "description": "The Great Stink of 1858: a bad smell saved London. In the hot summer of 1858 the River Thames was an open sewer, and the stench reached the Houses of Parliament on its bank.\n\nThe curtains were soaked in chloride of lime, and some MPs wanted to move Parliament out of London. Cholera had killed tens of thousands of Londoners, yet the sewer plans went nowhere. Then the stink did it: Benjamin Disraeli's bill became law in 18 days, and Joseph Bazalgette built the sewers that still run under London.\n\nSubscribe to Stickstory for one strange true story a day. Would you have stayed in Parliament that summer? Tell me in the comments!\n\n#shorts #greatstink #londonhistory #stickstory",
